Changes code from Helium's helium/core/noise/canvas.patch (GPL-3.0, The Helium Authors).

Helium's canvas noise moves every channel of a pixel it noises by up to 2, including channels at exactly 0 or 255
(it only held alpha there). Pages read those exact values back as data. Google Maps' vector map draws its road
shields from 9-patch PNGs (shield-*.9.png) and finds their stretch areas by reading back the 1px border, where every
marker is opaque pure black. With noise on, about one marker pixel in seven came back as (2,0,1) or (0,0,2), the
9-patch didn't parse, and the whole shield was scaled up as one bitmap: blurry, twice the size, with the number
squeezed into its top. Colour keys, masks and QR or barcode readers that compare against 0 and 255 fail the same way,
and flat white or black fills picked up visible speckle.

A channel at 0 or 255 now keeps its exact value, and noise never moves another channel onto 0 or 255, so a page can
trust an exact 0 or 255 it reads back. Every other channel gets the same noise as before, from the same hash
sequence. That's where readback fingerprints come from: anti-aliased text and shape edges, gradients and blending.
The float readbacks (RGBA_F16, RGBA_F32) follow the same rule for channels that quantize to 0 or 255.

